# {{ name }}

Roblox project generated by [Rowork](https://github.com/nokogoat/Rowork) v{{ roworkVersion }}.

## Stack

- **roblox-ts**: TypeScript compiled to Luau
- **Flamework**: services, controllers, dependency injection
- **Rojo**: file syncing into Roblox Studio
- **Rokit**: pinned tool versions

## Getting started

`rowork init` already installed the npm dependencies and the pinned toolchain,
so there is nothing left to set up.

```bash
rowork dev           # compiler, Rojo server and sourcemap watcher, together
```

Connect the Rojo plugin in Studio (see [Getting started](https://github.com/nokogoat/Rowork/blob/main/docs/getting-started.md))
and your code shows up there. `rowork dev` also prints a local dashboard address: open it to see
the project and the live logs.

## Building your game

Two kinds of commands, and it is worth knowing which is which before you reach for either:

- **`rowork add`**: a whole feature, ready-made (saved player data, a leaderboard, typed
  client/server messages, a React interface...). Run `rowork add` alone to see the list and pick
  one; each one asks its own questions. Once per feature.
- **`rowork make`**: one file of your own, in the right place, already wired in (a service, a
  screen, a saved value...). Run `rowork make` alone to see the list. As many times as you like.

A common order for a new game:

```bash
rowork add                 # player data, a leaderboard, typed networking, an interface...
rowork make:stat kills     # a value saved per player, added everywhere it needs to be
rowork make:screen Shop    # a screen of your interface
rowork make:service Vault  # your own server-side logic
```

Every one of these has a guided version (run it with no arguments) and a scripted one (pass
arguments and flags). `rowork info` lists everything installed and available, and
`rowork console` is a prompt where you can try commands one after another.

## Layout

```
src/
  server/services/       Flamework services (server-side logic)
  client/controllers/    Flamework controllers (client-side logic)
  shared/                code shared between server and client
```

`runtime.server.ts` and `runtime.client.ts` are the entry points: they declare
which directories to scan, then call `Flamework.ignite()`.

## Rowork configuration

`rowork.json` describes the project paths and the active plugins. `tsconfig.json`,
`default.project.json` and `package.json` remain standard files you can edit
directly: Rowork never hides them.
